<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Cimino</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Cimino is the highest peak, at about 1,050 metres, of the Monti Cimini, a range of extinct volcanoes near Viterbo in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In antiquity the range was covered by the dense Ciminian Forest, which the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> described as a feared barrier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> until a Roman army crossed it in 310 BCE. Its slopes are still known for their ancient beech woods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-cimino.jpg" alt="Monte Cimino" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dino Michelini · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Cimino_versante_nord.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
